%description:

RFC 826 -- a packet for a protocol the receiver does not speak provokes nothing.

Checks RFC826-RECV-3 from doc/project/evidence/standard/rfc826/catalog.md. The English
procedure is in
doc/project/evidence/protocol/arp/checks/input-validation.md#an-unknown-protocol-space;
the program steps below map onto its expected observations. Mapping of the shared link
mockup: host A = hostA, host B = hostB, host C = hostC, switch S = switch.

Every field of the crafted packet is that of a valid request from host A to host B, except
the protocol space, which is 0x86DD, the Ethertype of IPv6. The hosts run IPv4 and nothing
else. The protocol length stays 4, so the four address fields keep the widths the packet
actually has, and the target protocol address is the address of host B, so the only reason
to discard the packet is the one under test: the second question of the reception algorithm
of RFC 826, "Do I speak the protocol in ar$pro?".

The protocol space value is a real one and not an invented number. A station may meet it on
a link where a second protocol runs, and it must then answer for that protocol or stay
silent, never answer for IPv4.

The packet is built octet by octet and not from field values. The protocol space is not a
field of the packet class of the simulation model, which carries the value implicitly, so
only the octets can hold a value other than the one for IPv4.

Defect. This test FAILS and the failure is NOT declared expected, because the model claims the
behaviour: ArpPacketSerializer::deserializeFields tests this very field and calls markIncorrect
when it does not hold (ArpPacketSerializer.cc:52 to 55). So the model detects the condition RFC 826
asks it to detect, and what it does next is wrong -- Arp::processArpPacket peeks the packet with no
flags and the run stops. A mechanism that exists and does the wrong thing is a defect.

What the run shows. The model reads the octets with ArpPacketSerializer, whose
deserializeFields marks the packet incorrect when the protocol space is not the Ethertype of
IPv4 (src/inet/networklayer/arp/ipv4/ArpPacketSerializer.cc), and Arp::processArpPacket then
peeks the packet, which stops the run with
"Returning an incorrect chunk is not allowed according to the flags: 0".
RFC 826 asks for a discard and an end of processing, and the run never reaches the end. The
model reaches the same outcome for a wrong hardware space, which
Rfc5494ExperimentalHardwareSpace.test records.
See doc/project/evidence/model/arp/results.md.

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"
#include "ArpMutations.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

static Packet *buildUnknownProtocolSpacePacket(const CaptureStore&)
{
    return buildRawArpPacket("craftedProtocolSpace", "hostB.eth[0]",
            1, ETHERTYPE_IPv6, 6, 4, 1,
            interfaceMacAddress("hostA.eth[0]"), interfaceIpv4Address("hostA.eth[0]"),
            MacAddress::UNSPECIFIED_ADDRESS, interfaceIpv4Address("hostB.eth[0]"));
}

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("rfc826_unknown_protocol_space")
        // Expected observation 1: the crafted packet arrives at host B, protocol space
        // 0x86DD.
        .inject(inject("hostB").into("eth[0]", "upperLayerOut").at(0.5)
                  .describe("an ARP packet with the protocol space of IPv6, addressed to host B")
                  .packet(buildUnknownProtocolSpacePacket))
        // Expected observations 2 and 3: host B puts no ARP packet on the link afterwards,
        // and host B goes on working to the end of the run (RFC826-RECV-3, RECV-1).
        .never(on("hostB")
                  .match([](const MatchContext& c) { return arpPacketLeaves(c.event, "hostB"); })
                  .describe("any ARP packet from host B")
                  .within(0.4));
}

%activity:
// present only so opp_test compiles the %global above; this module is not in the network.
EV << "rfc826_unknown_protocol_space compiled\n";

%file: test.ned

import inet.node.ethernet.Eth100M;
import inet.node.ethernet.EthernetSwitch;
import inet.node.inet.StandardHost;
import inet.networklayer.configurator.ipv4.Ipv4NetworkConfigurator;

// The shared link mockup: three hosts on one Ethernet segment, joined by a switch.
network ArpSharedNet
{
    submodules:
        configurator: Ipv4NetworkConfigurator { @display("p=100,50"); }
        switch: EthernetSwitch { @display("p=400,150"); }
        hostA: StandardHost { @display("p=250,100"); }
        hostB: StandardHost { @display("p=250,250"); }
        hostC: StandardHost { @display("p=550,150"); }
    connections:
        hostA.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> switch.ethg++;
        hostB.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> switch.ethg++;
        hostC.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> switch.ethg++;
}

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
network = ArpSharedNet
sim-time-limit = 1s
seed-set = 0

# No host sends a datagram: the crafted packet is the only stimulus of the run.

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST rfc826_unknown_protocol_space: PASS
